WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.091
Long context, caching, or RAG?

2
00:00:02.371 --> 00:00:06.659
Current frontier models accept very long inputs (Claude Opus 5.

3
00:00:06.939 --> 00:00:13.744
5 has a one-million-token context window), so a fair question for any corpus is: why retrieve at all?

4
00:00:14.024 --> 00:00:33.544
Anthropic’s own contextual retrieval write-up (September 2024) gives a simple rule of thumb: if the knowledge base is under about 200,000 tokens (around 500 pages), you can include all of it in the prompt, and prompt caching makes that much cheaper and faster.

5
00:00:33.824 --> 00:00:35.851
Compare the options on four axes.

6
00:00:36.131 --> 00:00:36.877
Cost.

7
00:00:37.157 --> 00:00:40.699
Without caching, you pay for the whole corpus on every question.

8
00:00:40.979 --> 00:00:46.227
With caching, the corpus is a stable prefix: you pay a cache write when it’s created (1.

9
00:00:46.507 --> 00:00:54.251
25 times the input price for the five-minute lifetime) and a much lower read price afterwards (on Claude Opus 5.

10
00:00:54.531 --> 00:00:55.640
5, 0.

11
00:00:55.920 --> 00:00:59.824
05 times the input price at the time of writing; 0.

12
00:01:00.104 --> 00:01:01.981
1 times on many other models).

13
00:01:02.261 --> 00:01:06.933
Each hit refreshes the lifetime for free, so steady traffic keeps the cache warm.

14
00:01:07.213 --> 00:01:08.109
Latency.

15
00:01:08.389 --> 00:01:11.547
Processing a long prompt takes time even when it’s cheap.

16
00:01:11.827 --> 00:01:21.704
Caching helps a lot here (Anthropic reported more than a twofold latency reduction in that write-up), but a RAG prompt of a few thousand tokens is still shorter.

17
00:01:21.984 --> 00:01:22.944
Accuracy.

18
00:01:23.224 --> 00:01:34.147
Long context removes retrieval misses, ranking misses, and context overflow in one stroke, which is why it often wins on average in studies like Li and colleagues’.

19
00:01:34.427 --> 00:01:41.637
It doesn’t remove synthesis errors, and “lost in the middle” effects mean the model can still overlook a passage it was given.

20
00:01:41.917 --> 00:01:50.579
Barnett and colleagues also noted that larger context windows improved answer accuracy in their systems, which is consistent with this.

21
00:01:50.859 --> 00:01:52.117
Freshness and scale.

22
00:01:52.397 --> 00:01:57.475
Every change to the corpus invalidates the cached prefix after the point of change.

23
00:01:57.755 --> 00:02:06.096
Corpora that are large, change often, or need per-user permissions (each user sees different documents) push you toward retrieval.

24
00:02:06.376 --> 00:02:10.813
The conclusion is not “RAG is obsolete” or “long context is a gimmick.

25
00:02:11.093 --> 00:02:24.043
” It’s that for a small, stable corpus, long context with caching is the baseline your RAG system must beat on your eval set, and for a large or fast-changing one, RAG is the only option that scales.
